# modules/dast/passive/checks-leakage.rules - the §7.1 information-disclosure
# script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md
# DAST-10, tier 2).  Owned by modules/dast/passive/leakage.sh; ids are all
# `DAST-LEAK-*`.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, WHICH IS WHAT rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9'S
# `checks-<name>.rules` ROW EXISTS FOR.  This directory's registry used to be a
# single co-owned `checks.rules` that every tier-2 passive ticket appended to,
# because the §9 path table reserved only that one basename and any other name
# was `E070` - measured, on `modules/dast/passive/cookies.rules`, by
# `tests/lint-rules.sh` itself.  DAST-05..DAST-11 are peers with no ordering
# between them, so any two built in parallel produced an add/add conflict on that
# one file, and the mitigation ("take both sides") was a convention that has to be
# re-taught to every author and is silently wrong exactly once before anyone
# notices.  §9 now also reserves the `checks-` PREFIX, so each ticket owns its own
# file and there is no shared file left to conflict on.
#
# Nothing about identity moved with these records.  §9.5.1's owning-module map
# keys on the DIRECTORY, so E018/E081 still hold every id here to the `DAST-`
# prefix; a check id is unchanged by the file it lives in, so E019 uniqueness is
# still repository-wide; and `checks_registry_load` (lib/checks.sh) globs every
# `*.rules` under `modules/dast/` at any depth with no per-file allowlist, so this
# file loads alongside its siblings with no registration step.  The records below
# were moved BYTE-IDENTICALLY out of the shared file: a check id feeds the finding
# fingerprint (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 5), so a split is a move and never an
# edit.
#
#
# FIVE IDS FOR FIVE FAMILIES, AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC.  `check_id` is a
# fingerprint component (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 5) and the DAST location
# profile carries no component naming the DEFECT, so a stack trace and a leaked
# email on one path would collide on one fingerprint under a single id and
# `findings_merge` would silently keep whichever won the sort.  They also carry
# genuinely different severities, CWEs and remediations, which one record cannot
# state.
#
# `intrusive` is deliberately NOT set on any of these: this phase issues plain
# GETs to URLs an earlier phase already fetched and provokes nothing, so the
# passive tier is the whole gate and requiring --allow-intrusive on top would be
# a second, undocumented one.

id: DAST-LEAK-STACK_TRACE-01
title: Response body discloses a server stack trace or debugger page
script: passive/leakage.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-209
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Turn the framework debug flag off in every deployed environment and
  serve a generic error document instead, with the detail written to a
  server-side log the visitor cannot read. Treat this as a configuration default
  rather than a per-page fix: the same flag governs every handler, and an
  interactive debugger page frequently also exposes an evaluation console, which
  turns disclosure into remote code execution. Verify by requesting a path that
  is known to raise and confirming the response carries no filesystem path, no
  line number and no framework version.
references: CWE-209
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-LEAK-PROXY_HEADER-01
title: Response header discloses internal infrastructure
script: passive/leakage.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Strip the header at the edge before the response leaves the
  perimeter, rather than at the application, so a route that bypasses the
  application is covered too. Most reverse proxies add these for internal
  debugging and have a directive to unset them on egress. An internal address or
  an internal DNS name is reconnaissance for a later server-side request forgery
  or a lateral move: it tells an attacker the private range in use, the naming
  convention, and often the orchestration platform.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-LEAK-EMAIL-01
title: Email address disclosed outside a published contact link
script: passive/leakage.sh
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A01:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Remove the address from the served bytes where it is incidental - a
  leftover HTML comment, a serialised record in an embedded JSON blob, or a
  bundled test fixture. A named individual address harvested this way is the raw
  material for a targeted phishing or password-reset attempt, and it is also a
  username on many systems. Where an address must be published, publish a role
  alias on a contact page rather than an individual mailbox: this check
  deliberately does not report addresses the site links as mailto:, which is the
  publication route it treats as intentional.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-LEAK-JS_CONFIG-01
title: Served JavaScript carries a credential or internal configuration value
script: passive/leakage.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-540
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Treat the value as compromised and rotate it before anything else:
  every visitor since the bundle was published has had it. Then move the secret
  server-side and have the browser call an endpoint that holds it, rather than
  shipping it. Audit the build pipeline for the cause, which is usually a whole
  environment file inlined into the bundle rather than one deliberate variable -
  so assume siblings. Keep only values a vendor documents as browser-safe (a
  publishable key, an OAuth client id, an analytics id) in client code; this
  check allow-lists those by name and by prefix and does not report them.
references: CWE-540
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-LEAK-THIRD_PARTY_ORIGIN-01
title: Page loads content from third-party origins
script: passive/leakage.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-829
owasp: A08:2021
tags: passive
coverage-scope: target
remediation: This is an inventory rather than a defect: review the list and
  confirm each origin is one the application means to depend on. Each
  third-party origin serving active content can execute script in this page
  context, so its compromise is this application compromised. Reduce the list
  where a dependency can be self-hosted, pin what remains with Subresource
  Integrity, and constrain the set with a Content-Security-Policy that names
  exactly these origins - so an origin added later, by an attacker or by an
  unreviewed change, is blocked rather than silently trusted.
references: CWE-829
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/
